<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Privacy manifest for the containing app.

  WHY THIS EXISTS. Apple requires a reason to be declared for a small set of
  "required reason" APIs, and this project uses two of them, both from
  `AIKeyboardShared`, which all three targets link:

    * System boot time — `CaptureClock` (`CapturePages.swift`) is
      `clock_gettime_nsec_np(CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW)` everywhere, and
      `KeyboardPresence.bootIdentity` reads `kern.boottime`. Both are used to
      measure elapsed time between events inside the app, and boot time is used
      for equality only, never arithmetic. A wall clock was rejected on purpose:
      a clock the user can set is a clock that can make a stale reading look
      fresh.
    * User defaults — `SharedStore` and `SharedContainer` read and write the App
      Group suite so the app, the keyboard extension and the broadcast upload
      extension see one store.

  There is no manifest anywhere in this project's history, so an App Store
  submission would have been rejected for the boot-time call alone.

  TWO THINGS TO CHECK BEFORE SUBMITTING, because neither could be verified here.

  1. THE REASON CODES BELOW WERE NOT READ OFF APPLE'S DOCUMENTATION. Apple's doc
     pages render client-side and could not be fetched from this machine, and
     Xcode 26.2 does not ship the code list in any searchable resource. `35F9.1`
     and `1C8F.1` are believed correct — `35F9.1` for measuring elapsed time
     between in-app events, `1C8F.1` for user defaults shared across an App
     Group — and they match this project's actual use, but check them against
     "Describing use of required reason API" before you upload. A wrong code is
     a rejection email, not a crash.

  2. THE COLLECTED-DATA SECTION IS A CLAIM ABOUT THE PRODUCT, NOT ABOUT THE
     CODE, AND IT NEEDS THE OWNER'S EYES. What the code does is unambiguous:
     when a backend URL is configured, `BackendTransport` posts the text being
     rewritten to `/v1/text`, and `CloudScreenReader` posts a JPEG of the screen
     to `/v1/screen`. Both leave the device, so both are "collected" in Apple's
     sense. They are declared below as other user content, used only for app
     functionality, not linked to identity and not used for tracking — which is
     what this code does today. If the backend ever logs, retains, or trains on
     what it receives, this file becomes false and has to change with it.

  Kept identical in all three targets. `AIKeyboard/`, `AIKeyboardExtension/` and
  `AIKeyboardBroadcast/` are `PBXFileSystemSynchronizedRootGroup`s, so a file
  dropped in each is bundled with no `.pbxproj` edit.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategorySystemBootTime</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>35F9.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>1C8F.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUserContent</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
